al was part of multiple families (and multiple family units at different levels of inclusion), each participated in multiple religions. It is only from an outsider’s perspective, that all Orokaiva (in precolonial times) practiced a single religion. Arguably, it was a single type of religion, but then one could say that as well about Islam and Christianity. The coexistence of numerous locally-specific Orokaiva precolonial religions is intrinsically tolerant and pluralist, so, given that this is a project about religious tolerance, that configuration of "religion" should perhaps find representation." (Ira Bashkow, pers. comm. to R. Ainsworth, 26 April 2025)'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 947, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 769})
